/-!
# Commutation-aware cancellation

A gate list is read as a list of items: a single gate, or an X-block `H q; D; H q` with
`D` a nonempty run of gates diagonal on `q`. Each self-inverse gate (`H`, `X`, `Y`, `Z`,
`CNOT`, `CZ`), each `T`/`T†` and each `RzQ` scans forward over the items it commutes
with, up to `commuteWindow` items, until it meets its inverse (both are deleted) or, for
`RzQ`, a rotation on the same qubit (the two merge, and vanish when the angles sum to
`0`). The scan stops at the first item that does not commute.

Commutation is decided on atoms. Each item's matrix lies in the algebra generated by
one-qubit embeddings of three kinds: diagonal matrices, X-type matrices `H D H` with `D`
diagonal, and arbitrary matrices. A `CNOT c t` is `|0⟩⟨0|_c + |1⟩⟨1|_c X_t`, so its atoms
are a diagonal atom on `c` and an X-type atom on `t`; a `CZ a b` has two diagonal atoms; an
X-block on `q` is one X-type atom. Two atoms commute when they sit on different qubits or
have the same kind other than arbitrary, and two items commute when all their atoms do
(`gen_commute`). This covers disjoint gates, diagonal gates on a shared qubit, a diagonal
gate on a `CNOT` control, `X` and X-blocks on a `CNOT` target, and two `CNOT`s sharing a
control or a target.

## Main definitions

* `commuteCancelList`, `commuteCancel` — the pass on gate lists and on circuits.

## Main results

* `unitaryDenote_commuteCancel` — the pass preserves `unitaryDenote`.
* `gateCount_commuteCancel_le` — the pass does not increase the gate count.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace CatCrypt.Quantum.Optimization.CommuteCancel

open scoped Matrix
open CatCrypt.Quantum.QCircuit CatCrypt.Quantum.GateUnitarity
open CatCrypt.Quantum.Programs.Qubit CatCrypt.Quantum.UnitaryChannel
open CatCrypt.Quantum.Optimization.MixedCommute (IsDiagonal isDiagonal_pauliZ)
open CatCrypt.Quantum.ControlledGates (ctrlMatrix isDiagonal_proj0 isDiagonal_proj1
